Ladybird keeps every storage bottle's map in the browser process, in a
StorageJar, and a StorageAreaRegistry there keys each bottle of each
jar by a StorageAreaId and holds the set of WebContent processes
connected to it. The jar reports every change it applies to an
observer the registry installs, so a change made by a page, by
DevTools or by clearing site data fans out to every connected process
without any of those callers having to broadcast. Blink's browser-side
StorageAreaImpl is the same object: it owns the observer set its
renderers' CachedStorageAreas bind through AddObserver, and relays
every mutation to them. WebKit's StorageAreaBase in the network
process keeps the listener map its StorageAreaMaps register in, and
its dispatchEvents and notifyListenersAboutClear reach them all. Gecko
has no listener set of this shape: its parent-side Datastore notifies
the other processes' Snapshots key by key, and its LSObserver actors
carry the storage events.

An area ID is a claim about the sender, checked the way a page ID is,
and one ever issued to a connection stays valid for the connection's
life, so a write that races a browser-side teardown is dropped rather
than treated as misbehavior.
